1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,510
In this video, I'm going
to talk about MQTT and

2
00:00:03,510 --> 00:00:05,940
some IoT platforms
if you want to

3
00:00:05,940 --> 00:00:10,335
create a IoT-based Linux device.

4
00:00:10,335 --> 00:00:14,175
I'm going to introduce
the MQTT protocol,

5
00:00:14,175 --> 00:00:18,115
understand how to use security
with MQTT-based products,

6
00:00:18,115 --> 00:00:19,920
and we'll also introduce

7
00:00:19,920 --> 00:00:22,740
the JSON format
and the concept of

8
00:00:22,740 --> 00:00:25,470
device shadows or digital
twins that are common

9
00:00:25,470 --> 00:00:29,380
with some of the IoT platforms
that are available today.

10
00:00:29,960 --> 00:00:34,650
MQTT stands for Message
Queuing Telemetry Transport.

11
00:00:34,650 --> 00:00:36,270
It's a relatively old standard,

12
00:00:36,270 --> 00:00:38,535
it's been around since 1999.

13
00:00:38,535 --> 00:00:41,090
It consists of all
lightweight publish

14
00:00:41,090 --> 00:00:43,610
and subscribe messaging
transport that has

15
00:00:43,610 --> 00:00:46,850
some benefits in that it's
low bandwidth and it's

16
00:00:46,850 --> 00:00:51,690
pretty efficient distribution
to one or more devices.

17
00:00:51,690 --> 00:00:53,690
It's an efficient
way to distribute

18
00:00:53,690 --> 00:00:56,285
common messages across
different devices.

19
00:00:56,285 --> 00:01:01,270
It's pretty popular and
used on many IoT platforms.

20
00:01:01,270 --> 00:01:02,960
It also uses a lot of

21
00:01:02,960 --> 00:01:05,600
the same transport
that you use for HTTP.

22
00:01:05,600 --> 00:01:07,760
It can be securely
transmitted using

23
00:01:07,760 --> 00:01:10,640
SSL and TLS and
libraries that are

24
00:01:10,640 --> 00:01:14,300
typically available for
web-connected devices.

25
00:01:14,300 --> 00:01:16,100
There are protocols that

26
00:01:16,100 --> 00:01:17,480
are available for
establishing and

27
00:01:17,480 --> 00:01:21,020
authenticating the encrypted
links between devices.

28
00:01:21,020 --> 00:01:22,490
Again, it's that same technology

29
00:01:22,490 --> 00:01:24,560
behind HTTPS and how you

30
00:01:24,560 --> 00:01:25,775
connect with your bank account

31
00:01:25,775 --> 00:01:28,575
over a web browser for instance.

32
00:01:28,575 --> 00:01:31,410
There are open-source
applications, for instance,

33
00:01:31,410 --> 00:01:34,790
the Mosquito client-server
open-source software

34
00:01:34,790 --> 00:01:37,040
is available at this link and

35
00:01:37,040 --> 00:01:42,870
able to be included in a build.

36
00:01:42,870 --> 00:01:44,735
I wanted to use as an example,

37
00:01:44,735 --> 00:01:46,940
maybe you'd have
a door or window

38
00:01:46,940 --> 00:01:51,860
sensors in an embedded
device implementation where

39
00:01:51,860 --> 00:01:54,830
there's a way that you're
interacting with these through

40
00:01:54,830 --> 00:01:56,780
a custom Cloud server

41
00:01:56,780 --> 00:01:59,585
and a custom application
on a mobile device.

42
00:01:59,585 --> 00:02:02,615
Talking through how you
can use an IoT framework.

43
00:02:02,615 --> 00:02:05,330
This original set of slides

44
00:02:05,330 --> 00:02:08,060
was done to talk
about Amazon IoT

45
00:02:08,060 --> 00:02:11,300
in particular but there are
several frameworks you could

46
00:02:11,300 --> 00:02:13,370
use that all have
really similar ways

47
00:02:13,370 --> 00:02:15,620
to interact with the device.

48
00:02:15,800 --> 00:02:18,000
If we have a scenario

49
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:18,980
like this where we
want to be able to

50
00:02:18,980 --> 00:02:20,510
communicate with either
the mobile phone

51
00:02:20,510 --> 00:02:21,755
or the Cloud device,

52
00:02:21,755 --> 00:02:25,555
how can we leverage an IoT
platform to help us do this?

53
00:02:25,555 --> 00:02:28,685
First of all, talking
about how we will

54
00:02:28,685 --> 00:02:32,350
communicate between the
IoT device and the server,

55
00:02:32,350 --> 00:02:35,700
there's what's called a basic

56
00:02:35,700 --> 00:02:42,905
security scheme that you
would have with any device,

57
00:02:42,905 --> 00:02:45,410
IoT device, and server setup

58
00:02:45,410 --> 00:02:47,930
where the server would
have a certificate and

59
00:02:47,930 --> 00:02:50,300
a private key that
can be used to set up

60
00:02:50,300 --> 00:02:53,845
an encrypted communication
channel with the IoT device.

61
00:02:53,845 --> 00:02:56,360
Then the IoT device
would have some way to

62
00:02:56,360 --> 00:02:59,090
authenticate back over that
channel to the server.

63
00:02:59,090 --> 00:03:02,330
The key transfer that
you're doing here relies

64
00:03:02,330 --> 00:03:05,900
on the security of
encrypted channel.

65
00:03:05,900 --> 00:03:08,030
If someone is able to obtain

66
00:03:08,030 --> 00:03:09,960
this key that's transferred

67
00:03:09,960 --> 00:03:11,600
over the channel,
so for instance,

68
00:03:11,600 --> 00:03:14,630
a man in the middle
attack where you are able

69
00:03:14,630 --> 00:03:18,185
to spoof the server and get
you to pass the credentials,

70
00:03:18,185 --> 00:03:20,240
then you can emulate the device

71
00:03:20,240 --> 00:03:23,280
with the same permissions
on the server.

72
00:03:23,930 --> 00:03:26,159
However, with MQTT,

73
00:03:26,159 --> 00:03:29,710
there's an SSL security scheme
that allows you to set up

74
00:03:29,710 --> 00:03:32,125
a secure bi-directional
pipe with

75
00:03:32,125 --> 00:03:34,450
identity verified by

76
00:03:34,450 --> 00:03:37,270
a certificate authority,
signed certificate.

77
00:03:37,270 --> 00:03:39,700
The nice thing about
this is that you can set

78
00:03:39,700 --> 00:03:41,740
up a trust relationship
between the device and

79
00:03:41,740 --> 00:03:43,990
server without
having to transmit

80
00:03:43,990 --> 00:03:47,670
any private key information.

81
00:03:47,670 --> 00:03:49,620
This is a way that you can

82
00:03:49,620 --> 00:03:54,490
do if a hacker was able to
compromise this channel,

83
00:03:54,490 --> 00:03:56,590
they would not be able
to view anything that

84
00:03:56,590 --> 00:03:58,690
would allow them to
authenticate with

85
00:03:58,690 --> 00:04:01,000
the server because for
that they'd really need

86
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:04,385
the certificate that was
stored on the IoT device.

87
00:04:04,385 --> 00:04:09,250
It's an additional level
of security that's

88
00:04:09,250 --> 00:04:11,710
available to an
MQTT implementation

89
00:04:11,710 --> 00:04:15,560
based on the way
SSL can be set up.

90
00:04:16,650 --> 00:04:20,110
I'll talk a little bit
about the JSON format as

91
00:04:20,110 --> 00:04:23,245
a way that you can
communicate over MQTT.

92
00:04:23,245 --> 00:04:26,095
JSON stands for JavaScript
Object Notation,

93
00:04:26,095 --> 00:04:27,850
and it was originally
developed as part of

94
00:04:27,850 --> 00:04:30,875
the JavaScript programming
language standard.

95
00:04:30,875 --> 00:04:33,100
However, it's
language independent.

96
00:04:33,100 --> 00:04:35,160
It doesn't require you
to use JavaScript.

97
00:04:35,160 --> 00:04:37,690
It was just developed for
use with that standard.

98
00:04:37,690 --> 00:04:39,610
It's text-based, it's easier to

99
00:04:39,610 --> 00:04:42,705
read from a human
being perspective.

100
00:04:42,705 --> 00:04:45,490
It's organized in
objects so it's

101
00:04:45,490 --> 00:04:48,705
a set of name and value
pairs surrounded by braces.

102
00:04:48,705 --> 00:04:51,170
There's a specific format
with an object name followed

103
00:04:51,170 --> 00:04:54,125
by a colon and then
separated by a comma.

104
00:04:54,125 --> 00:04:57,085
It's easy to parse by
embedded software.

105
00:04:57,085 --> 00:05:01,125
Because of the fixed
and predictable format,

106
00:05:01,125 --> 00:05:03,260
it's pretty easy to
write parsers that are

107
00:05:03,260 --> 00:05:05,884
able to convert that text into

108
00:05:05,884 --> 00:05:08,030
instructions for software and

109
00:05:08,030 --> 00:05:12,570
especially embedded software,
relatively low resource.

110
00:05:12,590 --> 00:05:16,415
Here's an example about
how you could use

111
00:05:16,415 --> 00:05:21,230
Amazon IoT and MQTT to
transfer JSON messages.

112
00:05:21,230 --> 00:05:23,659
Using our example implementation

113
00:05:23,659 --> 00:05:24,950
where we have this mansion

114
00:05:24,950 --> 00:05:28,820
here and we're going to detect
whether a door is open,

115
00:05:28,820 --> 00:05:31,445
you could map this into

116
00:05:31,445 --> 00:05:34,100
a type of JSON response that
looks something like this,

117
00:05:34,100 --> 00:05:35,840
where there's a state

118
00:05:35,840 --> 00:05:37,700
where we're going to
report the state of

119
00:05:37,700 --> 00:05:39,710
the front door being
open and you can read

120
00:05:39,710 --> 00:05:42,365
this as the content of JSON.

121
00:05:42,365 --> 00:05:46,460
This is all formatted in
a way that is parsable

122
00:05:46,460 --> 00:05:48,140
by the embedded device or by

123
00:05:48,140 --> 00:05:51,300
the server and also
human-readable.

124
00:05:51,500 --> 00:05:54,560
What this slide is showing
is a case where we

125
00:05:54,560 --> 00:05:57,170
detected that the
door was open but

126
00:05:57,170 --> 00:05:58,790
maybe the person who owns

127
00:05:58,790 --> 00:06:00,740
the mansion is flying
in their private jet

128
00:06:00,740 --> 00:06:04,610
and they don't have connectivity
with their mobile phone,

129
00:06:04,610 --> 00:06:05,810
so they aren't going to get

130
00:06:05,810 --> 00:06:07,595
the message that
the device is open.

131
00:06:07,595 --> 00:06:11,330
However, with the IoT
server in the Cloud,

132
00:06:11,330 --> 00:06:13,220
that state's going to
get passed through to

133
00:06:13,220 --> 00:06:15,170
the server and it's
going to get updated

134
00:06:15,170 --> 00:06:17,060
on the server and maybe send

135
00:06:17,060 --> 00:06:21,030
some notifications as
needed from the server.

136
00:06:21,530 --> 00:06:25,490
What will happen is
at some later point,

137
00:06:25,490 --> 00:06:27,560
the mobile device comes online.

138
00:06:27,560 --> 00:06:29,420
At that point, the mobile
device could check in with

139
00:06:29,420 --> 00:06:32,855
the IoT server and it can
get an update on the state.

140
00:06:32,855 --> 00:06:35,840
The IoT server can give

141
00:06:35,840 --> 00:06:38,870
an update that the state
was reported as open and it

142
00:06:38,870 --> 00:06:40,670
can provide some
additional metadata

143
00:06:40,670 --> 00:06:41,840
that says how long it's been

144
00:06:41,840 --> 00:06:45,370
open when it was originally
reported by the device.

145
00:06:45,370 --> 00:06:48,815
One thing to note is that
for this message to happen,

146
00:06:48,815 --> 00:06:51,560
there does not necessarily
need to be connectivity here.

147
00:06:51,560 --> 00:06:56,030
It could be that the power is
switched off, for instance,

148
00:06:56,030 --> 00:06:59,000
in the mansion but the fact that

149
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:01,520
the AWS IoT endpoint is

150
00:07:01,520 --> 00:07:02,840
available means that
we can still get

151
00:07:02,840 --> 00:07:05,280
the update from
the mobile device.

152
00:07:06,440 --> 00:07:11,210
At this point, the
mobile phone user

153
00:07:11,210 --> 00:07:12,275
might want to say, well,

154
00:07:12,275 --> 00:07:15,275
if we had control of the
door through our device,

155
00:07:15,275 --> 00:07:17,540
then we'd want to issue

156
00:07:17,540 --> 00:07:20,990
a command to close the door
from the mobile device.

157
00:07:20,990 --> 00:07:23,390
Note in this case,
we still might

158
00:07:23,390 --> 00:07:25,280
not have connectivity
for whatever reason to

159
00:07:25,280 --> 00:07:28,085
the device but if

160
00:07:28,085 --> 00:07:30,595
we can relay that
message to the server,

161
00:07:30,595 --> 00:07:33,275
then the server can
save this state for us.

162
00:07:33,275 --> 00:07:35,920
Then at some point later
if the device comes back

163
00:07:35,920 --> 00:07:37,420
online and we're able to

164
00:07:37,420 --> 00:07:39,040
connect to it from
the server again,

165
00:07:39,040 --> 00:07:40,480
we can pass along

166
00:07:40,480 --> 00:07:42,775
that information about state

167
00:07:42,775 --> 00:07:44,320
that we are desiring
the front door

168
00:07:44,320 --> 00:07:46,030
closed and we can also have

169
00:07:46,030 --> 00:07:49,830
the server compute what's
called a Delta for us.

170
00:07:49,830 --> 00:07:51,820
In other words, the
server knows that

171
00:07:51,820 --> 00:07:54,730
the device reported that
the door was open and

172
00:07:54,730 --> 00:07:57,340
it knows that there's a
request to close it and so

173
00:07:57,340 --> 00:07:58,600
it can make it really easy for

174
00:07:58,600 --> 00:08:00,250
the device by
specifically saying,

175
00:08:00,250 --> 00:08:01,600
I want you to do this Delta.

176
00:08:01,600 --> 00:08:05,150
I want you to change the front
door from open to closed.

177
00:08:05,150 --> 00:08:07,960
The server can make it
a little bit easier on

178
00:08:07,960 --> 00:08:11,600
the device to know exactly
what to do in this scenario.

179
00:08:11,600 --> 00:08:15,040
Then, of course, the
device can relay

180
00:08:15,040 --> 00:08:17,590
back the state as
reported closed

181
00:08:17,590 --> 00:08:20,200
when it is closed
and that message can

182
00:08:20,200 --> 00:08:22,810
go back into the mobile
device and again,

183
00:08:22,810 --> 00:08:24,430
get a timestamp about
when it was actually

184
00:08:24,430 --> 00:08:27,225
closed from the target device.

185
00:08:27,225 --> 00:08:29,920
That's just an example about how

186
00:08:29,920 --> 00:08:32,470
a IoT-based interaction could

187
00:08:32,470 --> 00:08:36,475
happen on a platform
like Amazon IoT.

188
00:08:36,475 --> 00:08:40,540
There's several links here to
AWS tutorials that you can

189
00:08:40,540 --> 00:08:42,205
use if you wanted to try to use

190
00:08:42,205 --> 00:08:45,280
AWS IoT for your platforms.

191
00:08:45,280 --> 00:08:51,500
There's also very similar
services with similar concepts.

192
00:08:51,780 --> 00:08:57,290
Whereas Amazon refers to
these as Device Shadows,

193
00:08:57,290 --> 00:09:01,645
the Google Cloud IoT calls
this device registry,

194
00:09:01,645 --> 00:09:04,445
Watson IoT refers
to Digital twins,

195
00:09:04,445 --> 00:09:08,720
Azure IoT refers to Device
Shadows and they're all doing

196
00:09:08,720 --> 00:09:11,045
very similar things
and they're all using

197
00:09:11,045 --> 00:09:12,500
either MQTT or something

198
00:09:12,500 --> 00:09:15,275
similar for their
communication protocol.

199
00:09:15,275 --> 00:09:17,990
You can use the
basic concepts here

200
00:09:17,990 --> 00:09:20,390
to implement a project

201
00:09:20,390 --> 00:09:22,070
that uses some other kind of

202
00:09:22,070 --> 00:09:25,435
IoT-based framework
for your application.

203
00:09:25,435 --> 00:09:27,800
There's also, I wanted
to bring your attention

204
00:09:27,800 --> 00:09:30,470
to a student implementation that

205
00:09:30,470 --> 00:09:33,650
implemented a full Azure IoT

206
00:09:33,650 --> 00:09:36,255
as part of their final project.

207
00:09:36,255 --> 00:09:38,645
This is something you could
check out if you wanted

208
00:09:38,645 --> 00:09:42,410
to reuse any of their
work for Azure IoT.